WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.350
So, what are some of the
world's oldest institutions?

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:04.840
One of the oldest companies that we know

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:07.640
of is a temple construction
company called Kongo Gumi

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:10.880
which was started at the end
of the sixth century, at least.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.010
And it's still operating today.

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:14.029
Now, I believe it's been subsumed

00:00:14.029 --> 00:00:16.391
by a larger construction conglomerate

00:00:16.391 --> 00:00:18.300
but they're still building temples

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:21.500
and still largely the same
kind of operations and staff.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:25.010
One of the oldest hotels in
the world, also in Japan,

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:29.270
the Nishiyama Onsen Keiunkan,
kind of a Japanese homestay,

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:31.110
still in operation today.

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:33.770
One of the oldest restaurants
in the world built

00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:38.770
in a little after 800 A.D.
is the Stiftskeller St. Peter

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:40.861
Hotel and Restaurant.

00:00:40.861 --> 00:00:43.500
One of the oldest wineries that we know of

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:44.700
in the world, interestingly,

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:49.080
is the Staffelter Hof winery
in Germany built in 862,

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:50.290
as far as we know.

00:00:50.290 --> 00:00:52.350
Some of the other types of businesses

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:54.140
and operations that we start to see

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:57.270
over a millennia ago that
are still in operation

00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:59.000
are bars and pubs.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:01.600
So, Sean's bar in Ireland is said

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:04.013
to have been built in 900 A.D.

00:01:04.013 --> 00:01:05.830
The other thing that we start to see

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:07.780
in the 11th century is the invention

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:09.990
and proliferation of universities.

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:12.719
So we have things like
the University of Bologna

00:01:12.719 --> 00:01:16.640
and soon afterwards, the
University of Oxford, and then many

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:19.430
of the other universities
in the UK and other parts

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:21.380
of Europe really start to proliferate.

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:23.300
More recently we are seeing

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:24.611
some more commodity type things

00:01:24.611 --> 00:01:27.913
like the Poland Salt Mines in Wieliczka,

00:01:27.913 --> 00:01:30.350
starting in the 13th century.

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:33.252
And then even paper companies
like the Stora Enso

00:01:33.252 --> 00:01:35.835
Finnish paper company in 1288.

00:01:35.835 --> 00:01:37.900
But you'll notice that not only are many

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:39.260
of the oldest organizations

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:41.890
in the world in certain areas like Japan

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:43.840
and the old world of Europe,

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:45.590
but also there tend to be a ton

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:49.102
of breweries, winemakers, confectionaries,

00:01:49.102 --> 00:01:50.570
and some cases, hotels,

00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:53.120
organizations that
serve basic human needs.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.560
One of the other things that
you start to see when you look

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:58.760
at aggregates of companies
that's, for instance, lasts

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:02.370
for more than 200 years of
huge majority area in Japan.

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:04.330
So this idea that location matters,

00:02:04.330 --> 00:02:07.630
even though Japan has risen
and fallen and had lost wars,

00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:09.870
their basic system of government

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:12.420
and culture has lasted over time.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:14.810
And the other places, like
old-world Europe in Germany

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:16.780
and Holland and France are
some of the other places

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:19.543
that we see some of the
longest-lived institutions.

